    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d715">

                <pb facs="0770" n="760" sameAs="frus:frus1949v09/pg760"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/4–1849: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Shanghai</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Cabot</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Shanghai</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1949-04-18T17:00:00+08:00">April 18, 1949—5
                            p. m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received April 18—6:05 a. m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1235. [To Nanking:] ReContel’s 1216 to Department April 17, repeated Nanking 696,
                    Canton 273; 1234 to Department April 18, repeated Nanking 710, Canton
                        282.<anchor n="56" xml:id="d715-f1"/> Evidence thus far available suggests:
                    (1) That Acheson letter is welcomed by many politically alert Chinese in
                    Shanghai (where peace sentiment strong), (2) that local Chinese in general
                    accept letter as final proof that National Government will receive no more
                    substantial American aid, (3) that some Chinese financial and political
                    quarters, however, which have closely <pb facs="0771" n="761" xml:id="pg761"/>
                    studied text, persist in feeling that hope for possible aid is still justified
                    in light of letter’s penultimate sentence to effect that “further recommendation
                    might be made” if situation “clarifies itself sufficiently.” Sent Nanking 711,
                    repeated Department 1235, Canton 283.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Cabot</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d715-f1" n="56" xml:id="d715-f1-content">
                <p>Neither printed; they reported Chinese press comment on the Secretary of State’s
                    letter of March 15 to Senator Tom Connally, <ref target="#pg607">p.
                    607</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
